-- title:
--   L[x] is integrally closed for x transcendental
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be a field and let $F$ be a commutative ring which is an integral domain and carries an $L$-algebra structure. Let $x \in F$ be an element transcendental over $L$, that is, $x$ is not a root of any nonzero polynomial with coefficients in $L$ (in Mathlib's formulation, $x$ is not algebraic over $L$). The assertion is that the $L$-subalgebra $\operatorname{Algebra.adjoin} L \{x\} \subseteq F$ generated by $x$, regarded as a ring in its own right, is integrally closed: every element of its fraction field that is integral over it already lies in (the image of) the subalgebra. No hypothesis beyond transcendence of $x$ and $F$ being a domain over the field $L$ is imposed; in particular $F$ itself need not be a field, and the subalgebra is the concrete subobject $\operatorname{Algebra.adjoin} L \{x\}$ of $F$ rather than an abstract polynomial ring.
--
--   This is the standard fact that $L[x] \cong L[X]$ for $x$ transcendental, a principal ideal domain and hence integrally closed, stated in the form needed when $x$ is an element of an ambient domain. It is used in the treatment of curves and of models of modular curves, for instance in controlling the coefficients of minimal polynomials over such subalgebras and in constructing fibre models on cusp charts.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Subalgebra_isIntegrallyClosed_adjoin_singleton_of_transcendental.lean

import Mathlib

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
set_option synthInstance.maxHeartbeats 400000
